  'ser.coilsrc.slice': '★Use each slice\'s <strong>own</strong> raw Rogowski currents: physically each moment gets its own, but the solver treats the coil currents as <strong>exactly known</strong> and this shot\'s raw measurement is not that accurate — measured: at the reference time a fit on them diverges (singular normal equations) while the deck\'s converge (χ²/dof 1.5e-5). EFIT itself <strong>fits the coil currents too</strong>; this kernel does not, so this is a choice rather than a solved problem.',
  'ser.coilsrc.deck': '★Use the <strong>deck\'s</strong> (the delivered reconstruction\'s fitted coil currents at the reference time): they are self-consistent with that reconstruction\'s loop values and converge well — but they belong to the <strong>reference time</strong>, so using them on other slices puts one moment\'s vacuum field on another, which a current ramp will not stand.',
  'ser.deck.caveat': '★★This deck\'s nine slices are real (pulled from MDSplus and reduced by the kernel\'s own code), but **only the reference slice fits well**: the solver treats the coil currents as exactly known and the raw Rogowski measurement is not that accurate (2–9% per channel), and `pcrl01` is not the plasma current either (1.9% off at flat-top). Failed slices are reported as failures with their reason rather than given a plausible number. The measured comparison is in <code>docs/reference/fidelity.md</code>.',
