  // ======================================================================
  // T-D6 · the boundary class is an input (design page, configuration bar)
  // ======================================================================
  'design.class': 'Boundary class',
  'design.class.limiter': 'Limiter',
  'design.class.lsn': 'Lower single null (diverted)',
  'design.class.usn': 'Upper single null (diverted)',
  'design.class.dn': 'Double null (diverted)',
  'design.class_note': '★<strong>The boundary class is an input, not a sentence read off the answer.</strong> “Limiter” asks for <strong>no</strong> field null at all — <strong>bit for bit</strong> what this page gave before this control existed. A single null adds an X-point row automatically (B<sub>r</sub>=B<sub>z</sub>=0, with the null on the boundary\'s own flux surface); it goes where the <strong>device description declares its seed</strong>, and only a machine that declares none falls back to the target shape\'s own corner, (R₀ − δ·a, Z₀ ∓ κ·a). You can still move it by hand, or drag the × on the cross-section. ★<strong>“Double null” is now selectable (T-D18)</strong>. It used to be listed and disabled, naming the kernel entry the start design goes through — <code>fylite_rs_start_currents</code>, whose signature took exactly <strong>one</strong> field null (<code>xR</code>/<code>xZ</code>/<code>useX</code>). The kernel changed: both sides of the inverse solve now take a <strong>set</strong> of nulls (<code>fylite_rs_start_currents_multi</code>), each null still contributing three rows (its own isoflux row plus B<sub>r</sub>=B<sub>z</sub>=0). Asking for a double null asks for one above the requested axis and one below — two pairs of X-point fields on this panel, two draggable × on the section — and <strong>the strike criterion then requires 4 legs</strong>, because one X point sends out two. ★Once a single null is asked for, <strong>the class that comes back is judged</strong>: ask for a divertor, get a limiter, and the run has <strong>not reached what it was asked for</strong> — the criteria table, the strike-point block and this bar\'s status line all say so. ★<strong>Correction (EAST, 2026-08-23 second batch, T-D6′): the root cause of “cannot make a divertor” was the boundary rule, not the profile family.</strong> The fully converged reference-current solve read its nearest saddle at ψ<sub>N</sub> = 1.19 — but that number came from the <strong>naive max-flux rule</strong> reading a wall point in the private flux region: the bottleneck rule reads the same field\'s saddle at ψ<sub>N</sub> = 1.000. With kernel v108\'s FreeGS-style 0.75|Z<sub>x</sub>| guard on the contact candidates, <strong>the same reference currents and the same analytic family solve diverted</strong> (bndKind = 1, X at (1.58, −0.75)); the delivered p′/FF′ tier lands closer still to the delivered reconstruction — a 0.453 vs 0.440, R₀ 1.814 vs 1.814, X (1.62, −0.66) vs (1.61, −0.72). The profile half remains true (the delivered profiles change sign at ψ<sub>N</sub> ≈ 0.82, which the family cannot represent — see the current-profile source control), but it limits <strong>accuracy</strong>, not <strong>topology</strong>.',
