  'design.row.nulls': 'Field-null rows (above / below)',
  'design.mark.nulls_ok': 'the null rows are on the sides the class names',
  'design.mark.nulls_miss': '★a null row is not on the side the class names',
  // ======================================================================
  // T-D7 · gaps and strike points become rows of the inverse solve
  // ======================================================================
  'design.ctl': 'Shape-control rows (gaps / strike points)',
  'design.ctl_note': '★<strong>These are the quantities real shape control actually targets.</strong> This page could <strong>report</strong> the smallest wall clearance and where the legs landed, and could not target either — read-only, never a row of the inverse solve. Now it can. Both kinds become <strong>the same row</strong>: an isoflux row (“the boundary passes through here”) at a control point the <strong>wall</strong> defines, and <strong>both sides of the inverse solve carry it</strong> — the linear start design (<code>fylite_rs_start_currents_multi</code>) and every pass of the anneal. ★<strong>A gap</strong> is measured on a ray out of the target axis (R₀, Z₀) — <strong>outward / inward / up / down</strong>, the definition ROG and RIG already use — from where that ray meets the wall; the control point sits <em>gap</em> metres inside it. The ray depends only on the wall and the request, so it <strong>does not move while the solve runs</strong> and one response block serves the whole anneal. ★<strong>A strike point</strong> is an (R, Z) the kernel <strong>snaps onto a wall segment</strong> — it reports which segment and how far the request moved — and then asks the boundary flux surface to pass through it. ★The weight is relative to the boundary rows: raise it and the boundary hugs this row while the shape error gives ground. That is a <strong>trade-off, not a switch</strong>, and the 「目标 vs 实现」 table prints both halves. ★A row whose ray never meets the wall is <strong>named and reported</strong>, not silently dropped.',
